{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048499 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119805 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119616 :question/math-academy-id "e-4789" :question/problem "Find $s,$ if $3\\mathbf{a_1}+s\\mathbf{a_2} = \\mathbf{b}$ and\n\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a_1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{a_2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $s$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}+s{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+s\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that $s=4.$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120311 :question/math-academy-id "q-34249" :question/problem "Find $l$, if $5{a}_{1}+l{a}_{2}=b$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}45 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 19\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $l$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{a}_{1}+l{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 5\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+l\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}45 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 19\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ l\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}45 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 19\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ l\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that $l=-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120312 :question/math-academy-id "q-34250" :question/problem "Find $q$, if $7a_{1} + qa_{2} = b$ and\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}32 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240984 :question/math-academy-id "q-105152" :question/problem "Find $h$, if $3{a}_{1}+h{a}_{2}=b$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $h$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}+h{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 3\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+h\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ h\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ h\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that $h=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240985 :question/math-academy-id "q-105154" :question/problem "Find $m$, if $7{a}_{1}+m{a}_{2}=b$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $m$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{a}_{1}+m{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 7\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+m\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ m\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}-7\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ m\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that $m=-5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314967 :question/math-academy-id "q-34051" :question/problem "Find $k$, if $2{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=b$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $k$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+k\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that $k=-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317257 :question/math-academy-id "q-34241" :question/problem "Find $j$, if ${a}_{1}-j{a}_{2}=b$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $j$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}-j{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-j\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ j\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ j\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that $j=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319044 :question/math-academy-id "q-34570" :question/problem "Find $k$, if $3{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=0$ and \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $k$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 3\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+k\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the first components, we get \n\n$$\n3k=-6\\,⟹\\,k=-2.\n$$\n\nBut then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is no $k$ such that $3{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319045 :question/math-academy-id "q-34600" :question/problem "Find $l$, if $5{a}_{1}+l{a}_{2}=b$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -15 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $l$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{a}_{1}+l{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 5\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+l\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -15 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ l\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -15 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ l\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 15 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that \n$l=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319047 :question/math-academy-id "q-34602" :question/problem "Find $k$, if $8{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=0$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $k$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 8\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+k\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-8\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ -48 \\\\ 32\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the first components, we get \n\n$$\n-2k=24\\,⟹\\,k=-12.\n$$\n\nBut then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 48\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ -48 \\\\ 32\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is no $k$ such that \n $8{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419107 :question/math-academy-id "q-73923" :question/problem "Find $m$, if $5{a}_{1}+m{a}_{2}=0$ and \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $m$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{a}_{1}+m{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 5\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+m\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ m\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ m\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-30 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the first components, we get \n\n$$\n-5m=-30\\,⟹\\,m=6.\n$$\n\nBut then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-30 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\begin{bmatrix}-30 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is no $m$ such that $5{a}_{1}+m{a}_{2}=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542353 :question/problem "Find $k$, if $4{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=0$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $k$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 4\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+k\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the first components, we get\n\n$$\n2k=-8\\,⟹\\,k=-4.\n$$\n\nBut then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is no $k$ such that $4{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542366 :question/problem "Find $k$, if $3{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=0$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $k$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 3\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+k\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the first components, we get\n\n$$\n3k=-6\\,⟹\\,k=-2.\n$$\n\nBut then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is no $k$ such that $3{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562406 :question/math-academy-id "q-34572" :question/problem "Find $q$, if $2{a}_{1}+q{a}_{2}=b$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $q$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+q{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+q\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ q\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ q\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that $q=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562438 :question/math-academy-id "q-105156" :question/problem "Find $t$, if $2{a}_{1}+t{a}_{2}=0$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $t$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+t{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 2\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ t\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ t\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-16 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the first components, we get \n\n$$\n-4t=-16\\,⟹\\,t=4.\n$$\n\nBut then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-16 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\begin{bmatrix}-16 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is no $t$ such that \n $2{a}_{1}+t{a}_{2}=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562512 :question/math-academy-id "q-105153" :question/problem "Find $k$, if $6{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=b$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $k$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 6\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+k\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that $k=-2.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for One Coefficient in a Linear Combination"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119806 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119617 :question/math-academy-id "e-4790" :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{x},$ if $\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ and\n\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $\\mathbf{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+2x & =b \\\\ 2x & =b-a \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}(b-a) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ x & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120310 :question/math-academy-id "q-34248" :question/problem "Find $x$, if $4a+6x=b$ and\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4a+6x & =b \\\\ 6x & =b-4a \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{6}(b-4a) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}36 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ x & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120351 :question/math-academy-id "q-34574" :question/problem "Find the value of $x_{1} + x_{2}$ given that $2a - 6x = b$ and\n$a = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -22 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240986 :question/math-academy-id "q-105159" :question/problem "Find $x$, if $4a+3x=b$ and\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4a+3x & =b \\\\ 3x & =b-4a \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}(b-4a) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240987 :question/math-academy-id "q-105160" :question/problem "Find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}$ given that $3a-5x=b$ and\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-13 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a-5x & =b \\\\ -5x & =b-3a \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{5}(b-3a) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{5}(\\begin{bmatrix}-13 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-1+2=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240988 :question/math-academy-id "q-105161" :question/problem "Find the value of ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}$ given that $7a+4x=b$ and\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7a+4x & =b \\\\ 4x & =b-7a \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}(b-7a) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}-7\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=-2-1=-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314969 :question/math-academy-id "q-34052" :question/problem "Find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}$ given that $3a-5x=b$ and\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -22 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a-5x & =b \\\\ -5x & =b-3a \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{5}(b-3a) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{5}(\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -22 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3-1=-4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319046 :question/math-academy-id "q-34601" :question/problem "Find the value of ${x}_{3}-{x}_{4}$ given that  $6a-2x=b$ and\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a-2x & =b \\\\ -2x & =b-6a \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}(b-6a) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-16 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -48 \\\\ -58\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ x & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 24 \\\\ 29\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{3}-{x}_{4}=24-29=-5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342110 :question/math-academy-id "q-73931" :question/problem "Find $x$, if $3a+2x=b$ and\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $x,$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a+2x & =b \\\\ 2x & =b-3a \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}(b-3a) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ x & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342111 :question/math-academy-id "q-73933" :question/problem "Find $x$, if $5a-3x=b$ and\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 18 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $x,$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a-3x & =b \\\\ 3x & =5a-b \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}(5a-b) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}(5\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 18 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -48 \\\\ 51\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -16 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386134 :question/math-academy-id "q-105157" :question/problem "Find $x$, if $2a+4x=b$ and\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a+4x & =b \\\\ 4x & =b-2a \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}(b-2a) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391790 :question/math-academy-id "q-34247" :question/problem "Find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}$ given that $3a+2x=b$ and\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -15 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a+2x & =b \\\\ 2x & =b-3a \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}(b-3a) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -15 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ x & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=6+7=13.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393376 :question/math-academy-id "q-73936" :question/problem "Find $x$, if $4a-2x=b$ and\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $x,$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4a-2x & =b \\\\ 2x & =4a-b \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}(4a-b) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}(4\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 30 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 15 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393383 :question/math-academy-id "q-73937" :question/problem "Find $x$, if $2a+5x=b$ and\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -30 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a+5x & =b \\\\ 5x & =b-2a \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{5}(b-2a) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{5}(\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -30 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 40\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435824 :question/math-academy-id "q-34598" :question/problem "Find the value of ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}$ given that  $4a+3x=b$ and\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ -15 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4a+3x & =b \\\\ 3x & =b-4a \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}(b-4a) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ -15 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -27 \\\\ -21\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ x & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2-(-9)=11.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562449 :question/math-academy-id "q-105158" :question/problem "Find $x$, if $5a-2x=b$ and\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a-2x & =b \\\\ -2x & =b-5a \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}(b-5a) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ x & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Vector in a Linear Combination"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119807 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119618 :question/math-academy-id "e-4791" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c_1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{c_2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n\n\nIf $x_1 \\mathbf{c}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{c}_2  = \\mathbf d,$ then what is $x_1+x_2?$" :question/worked-solution "If $x_1 \\mathbf{c}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{c}_2  = \\mathbf d,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=5 \\\\ -{x}_{1} &=-5 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we obtain $x_1=5,$ which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & =5 \\\\ 4(5)+{x}_{2} & =5 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x_1=5$ and $x_2=-15$ into the third equation to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 3(5)+(-15) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a true statement. \n\nSo the solution of the system is $x_1=5$ and $x_2=-15,$ which means\n\n$$\n5 \\mathbf{c_1}-15 \\mathbf{c_2} = \\mathbf{d}.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nx_1+x_2=5-15=-10.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120293 :question/math-academy-id "q-34054" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{1} \\\\ -{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=5 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=3 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation we get ${x}_{2}=2$, which we substitute into the second equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & =3 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2 & =3 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=2$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & =5 \\\\ -(-1)+2(2) & =5 \\\\ 5 & =5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a true statement. \n\nSo the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=2,$ which means\n\n$$\n-{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}=b.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-1+2=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120350 :question/math-academy-id "q-34568" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,d=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}=d,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}=d,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{1} \\\\ 5{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=3 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=4 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $3$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13{x}_{2} & =13\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${x}_{2}=1$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+4(1) & =3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting ${x}_{1}=1$ and ${x}_{2}=1$ into the third equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & =5 \\\\ 5(1)+2(1) & =5 \\\\ 7 & =5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a false statement. \n\nSo, the system has no solution."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240990 :question/math-academy-id "q-105166" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -2{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ -3{x}_{2} \\\\ 5{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=-3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the resulting equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =-5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we substitute into the second equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-3(-5) & =1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1} & =-14 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute ${x}_{1}=7$ and ${x}_{2}=-5$ into the third equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ 3(7)+5(-5) & =-4 \\\\ -4 & =-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a true statement. \n\nSo the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=7$ and ${x}_{2}=-5,$ which means\n\n$$\n7{a}_{1}-5{a}_{2}=b.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=7+(-5)=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240991 :question/math-academy-id "q-105167" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{1} \\\\ -3{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-7{x}_{2} \\\\ 5{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{1}-7{x}_{2} \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-7{x}_{2} &=-9 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=3 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the first and the second equations we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} & =-6 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we substitute into the second equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+5(3) & =3 \\\\ -3{x}_{1} & =-12 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute ${x}_{1}=4$ and ${x}_{2}=3$ into the third equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ (4)-(3) & =1 \\\\ 1 & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a true statement. \n\nSo the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=4$ and ${x}_{2}=3,$ which means\n\n$$\n4{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2}=b.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4+3=7.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317259 :question/math-academy-id "q-34242" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,d=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}=d,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}=d,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0 &=4 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=5 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe first equation is a false statement. Hence,the system has no solution."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342116 :question/math-academy-id "q-73951" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,d=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}=d,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$\n." :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}=d,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=3 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=-11 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the second and the third  equations we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{1} & =-16\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${x}_{1}=-2$ into the third equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-2)+{x}_{2} &=-5\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting ${x}_{1}=-2$ and ${x}_{2}=3$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & =3 \\\\ (-2)+2(3) & =3 \\\\ 4 & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a false statement. \n\nSo, the system has no solution."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342117 :question/math-academy-id "q-73952" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=-7 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the first and the second equation we get\n\n$$\n-2{x}_{2}=-8\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${x}_{2}=4$ into the first equation yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} & =-7 \\\\ {x}_{1}-(4) & =-7 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-7+4 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute ${x}_{1}=-3$ and ${x}_{2}=4$ into the third equation to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} & =6 \\\\ 2(-3)+3(4) & =6 \\\\ 6 & =6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a true statement. \n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=-3$ and ${x}_{2}=4,$ which means\n\n$$\n-3{a}_{1}+4{a}_{2}=b.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3+4=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342118 :question/math-academy-id "q-73954" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5{x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{2} \\\\ -3{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=16 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=-18\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $3$, the second equation by $2$, and adding the resulting equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15{x}_{2}+8{x}_{2} & =48-2 \\\\ 23{x}_{2} & =46 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=2$  into the second equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} & =-1\\text{ } \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+4(2) & =-1 \\\\ 3{x}_{1} & =-9 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute ${x}_{1}=-3$ and ${x}_{2}=2$ into our third equation to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} & =-18 \\\\ 4(-3)-3(2) & =-18 \\\\ -18 & =-18,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a true statement. \n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=-3$ and ${x}_{2}=2,$ which means\n\n$$\n-3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}=b.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=(-3)+2=-1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342123 :question/math-academy-id "q-73981" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{1} \\\\ -2{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=7 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=9 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the third equation by $-1$ and adding the result to the first equation we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-3{x}_{1} & =7-4 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow,  we substitute ${x}_{1}=3$ into the third equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} & =4 \\\\ 3(3)-{x}_{2} & =4 \\\\ -{x}_{2} & =4-9 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute ${x}_{1}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=5$ into the second equation to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} & =9 \\\\ -2(3)+3(5) & =9 \\\\ -6+15 & =9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a true statement. \n\nSo the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=5,$ which means\n\n$$\n3{a}_{1}+5{a}_{2}=b.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=3+5=8.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435825 :question/math-academy-id "q-73950" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{2} \\\\ -3{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-11 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=-11 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=8 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=-14\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the second and the third equations we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1} & =-6 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we substitute into the second equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} & =8 \\\\ 4(-1)+3{x}_{2} & =8 \\\\ 3{x}_{2} & =12 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=4$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} & =-11 \\\\ 3(-1)-2(4) & =-11 \\\\ -11 & =-11,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a true statement. \n\nSo the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=4,$ which means\n\n$$\n-{a}_{1}+4{a}_{2}=b.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-1+4=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562514 :question/math-academy-id "q-105163" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}5{x}_{1} \\\\ 7{x}_{1} \\\\ -{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ 7{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=4 \\\\ 7{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=-11\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $(-2)$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1} & =-9\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${x}_{1}=3$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(3)+2{x}_{2} & =4\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting ${x}_{1}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{11}{2}$ into the third equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} & =-11 \\\\ -(3)+3(-\\frac{11}{2}) & =-11 \\\\ -\\frac{33}{2} & =-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a false statement. \n\nSo, the system has no solution."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573888 :question/math-academy-id "q-105165" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-8{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ -{x}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation we get ${x}_{2}=2$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2} & =-1 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-8(2) & =-1 \\\\ 3{x}_{1} & =15 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute ${x}_{1}=5$ and ${x}_{2}=2$ into the third equation to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} & =-2 \\\\ -2(5)+4(2) & =-2 \\\\ -2 & =-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a true statement. \n\nSo the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=5$ and ${x}_{2}=2,$ which means\n\n$$\n5{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}=b.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=5+2=7.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Two Coefficients in a Linear Combination"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119854 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119669 :question/math-academy-id "e-6191" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a_1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{a_2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{a_3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n\n\nIf $x_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf b,$ then what is $x_1+x_2+x_3?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{b}$ is a linear combination of vectors $\\mathbf{a_1}, \\,\\mathbf{a_2}$ and $\\mathbf{a_3}$ with coefficients $x_1,\\, x_2$ and $x_3,$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n\n\n$$\nM= \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 7\\end{array} \\right]\n$$\n\n\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $x_3 = 2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2+(2) = 1 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x_2 = -1,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x_3=2$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1+2(2) = 1 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x_1 = -3.\n$$\n\nSo the solution of the system is $x_1=-3, x_2=-1, x_3=2,$ which means\n\n$$\n-3 \\mathbf{a_1}- \\mathbf{a_2} +2 \\mathbf{a_3}= \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nx_1+x_2+x_3=-3-1+2=-2.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120309 :question/math-academy-id "q-34243" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b$, then we have  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=3 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3} &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=3 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-3$ and from the second equation we get ${x}_{2}=3.$ Substituting these into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3+2(-3)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=11.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=11$, ${x}_{2}=3$, and ${x}_{3}=-3,$ which means \n\n$$\n11{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2}-3{a}_{3}=b.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=11+3-3=11.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120349 :question/math-academy-id "q-34566" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ 5{x}_{2} \\\\ 6{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ 9{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=-6 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3} &=7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 9 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 9 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 9 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 13\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ {x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ 0 &=13.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation $0=13$  gives a false statement. So the system has no solution."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240992 :question/math-academy-id "q-105168" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b$, then we have  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{1} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ -8{x}_{2} \\\\ 6{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{3} \\\\ 5{x}_{3} \\\\ -3{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -8 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 6 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -8 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 6 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 6 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=-5 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=6.$ Substituting ${x}_{3}=6$ into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-6=-5\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${x}_{2}=1$ and ${x}_{3}=6$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3(1)+2(6)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-7.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=-7$, ${x}_{2}=1$, and ${x}_{3}=6,$ which means \n\n$$\n-7{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}+6{a}_{3}=b.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-7+1+6=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240993 :question/math-academy-id "q-105169" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 5{x}_{1} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ 6{x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{3} \\\\ -8{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3} &=-3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 6 & -8 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 6 & -8 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -13 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=-13 \\\\ 0 &=8.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation $0=8$  gives a false statement. So the system has no solution."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240994 :question/math-academy-id "q-105170" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{2} \\\\ -3{x}_{2} \\\\ 8{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}7{x}_{3} \\\\ -7{x}_{3} \\\\ 14{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}+14{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3} &=5 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3} &=-3 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}+14{x}_{3} &=7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 7 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -7 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 14 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 7 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -7 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 14 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 14 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3} &=5 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 0 &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation $0=-3$  gives a false statement. So the system has no solution."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240995 :question/math-academy-id "q-105172" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -5{x}_{1} \\\\ -3{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{2} \\\\ -21{x}_{2} \\\\ -12{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3{x}_{3} \\\\ 14{x}_{3} \\\\ 9{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\ -5{x}_{1}-21{x}_{2}+14{x}_{3} \\\\ -3{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}-21{x}_{2}+14{x}_{3} &=8 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3} &=-7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -21 & 14 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 9 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -21 & 14 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 9 & -7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & 18 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 9 & -7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & 18 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ -{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=18 \\\\ 0 &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation $0=-1$  gives a false statement. So the system has no solution."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342126 :question/math-academy-id "q-73992" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{3} \\\\ 5{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ 5{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=-5 \\\\ 5{x}_{3} &=-10 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & -10 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & -10 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -5 & 16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -5 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2.5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2.5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=-5 \\\\ {x}_{2}-2.5\\,{x}_{3} &=8 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-2.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-2.5(-2)=8\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=8-5=3.\n$$\n\nFinally,  substituting ${x}_{2}=3$ and ${x}_{3}=-2$  into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(3)+2(-2)=-5\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=2$, ${x}_{2}=3$, and ${x}_{3}=-2,$ which means \n\n$$\n2{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2}-2{a}_{3}=b.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=2+3-2=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342130 :question/math-academy-id "q-74006" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b,$ then   we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -{x}_{3} \\\\ -3{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ -{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=5 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-5 \\\\ 0 &=-12.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation $0=-12$  gives a false statement. So the system has no solution."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342132 :question/math-academy-id "q-74028" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-1 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-9 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-3 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(-1)=-3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, by substituting ${x}_{2}=-2,{x}_{3}=-1$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} & =-1 \\\\ {x}_{1}+(-2)+3(-1) & =-1 \\\\ {x}_{1}-5 & =-1 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=4$, ${x}_{2}=-2$, and ${x}_{3}=-1,$ which means \n\n$$\n4{a}_{1}-2{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=b.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=4+(-2)-(-1)=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342133 :question/math-academy-id "q-74032" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{3} \\\\ -11{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{2}-11{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=5 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}-11{x}_{3} &=-18 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -11 & -18 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -11 & -18 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -11 & -18 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -11 & -18\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -15 & -30\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{15}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=5 \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=-6 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=2.$ Substituting this value into  the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-2(2)=-6\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, by substituting ${x}_{2}=-2$ and ${x}_{3}=2$ into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} & =5 \\\\ {x}_{1}+(-2)+3(2) & =5 \\\\ {x}_{1}+4 & =5 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=1$, ${x}_{2}=-2$, and ${x}_{3}=2,$ which means \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}-2{a}_{2}+2{a}_{3}=b.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=1+(-2)+2=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342134 :question/math-academy-id "q-74035" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ 5{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ 5{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=-3 \\\\ 5{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=4 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0.2 & 0.8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=-{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0.2 & 0.8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=-3 \\\\ {x}_{2}+\\,0.2{x}_{3} &=0.8 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+0.2(-1)=0.8\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n   \n\nFinally,  substituting ${x}_{2}=1$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(1)=-3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=-2$, ${x}_{2}=1$, and ${x}_{3}=-1,$ which means  \n\n$$\n-2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=b.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-2+1-1=-2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342138 :question/math-academy-id "q-74046" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=8 \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=-6 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -14\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & -20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=8 \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=-6 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=5.$ Substituting this value in the second and first equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{2}-2(5)=-6\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=4 \\\\  & {x}_{1}+5=8\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=3$, ${x}_{2}=4$, and ${x}_{3}=5,$ which means \n\n$$\n3{a}_{1}+4{a}_{2}+5{a}_{3}=b.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=3+4+5=12.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371499 :question/math-academy-id "q-105171" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1} \\\\ -{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5{x}_{2} \\\\ -8{x}_{2} \\\\ 5{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=9 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -8 & 2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -8 & 2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=7 \\\\ 0 &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation $0=2$  gives a false statement. So the system has no solution."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573926 :question/math-academy-id "q-74011" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ -3{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ 0 &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nHowever, the final equation $0=2$  gives a false statement. So the system has no solution."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573932 :question/math-academy-id "q-74043" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ -5{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{3} \\\\ 11{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} \\\\ -5{x}_{2}+11{x}_{3} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ -5{x}_{2}+11{x}_{3} &=-4 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=15\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 11 & -4 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -2 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 11 & -4 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -2 & 15\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 11 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -14 & -7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -14 & -7 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 11 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{7}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 11 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=1.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-2(1)=1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, by substituting ${x}_{2}=3$ and ${x}_{3}=1$  into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(3)+3(1)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=5.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=5$, ${x}_{2}=3$, and ${x}_{3}=1,$ which means \n\n$$\n5{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=b.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=5+3+1=9.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Three Coefficients in a Linear Combination"}] :topic/title "Linear Combinations of Vectors in N-Dimensional Euclidean Space"}
